course: english
lesson: 57
title: Olympiad: riddles, lipograms and hidden words
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Riddles play with double meanings: "keys" can open locks, or be the black and white keys of a piano, and the riddle wants you to switch from one meaning to the other.
explain: Some riddles hide in the spelling: "ton" backwards is "not", and "short" becomes "shorter" when you add two letters, even though "shorter" is a longer word.
explain: Hidden-word puzzles tuck a word across the gaps between words: "Dad's car rots in the shed" hides CARROT, and "The pilot terrified everyone" hides OTTER.
explain: A lipogram is writing that leaves out one letter on purpose; leaving out "e", the commonest letter in English, is the hardest classic challenge.
explain: Picture Gran in Cork asking, "What word is spelled wrong in every dictionary, ya eejit?" ("eejit" means idiot, said fondly): the answer is "wrong", because the word "wrong" is always spelled W-R-O-N-G; read a riddle word for word, not just for its sense.
fact: "What has keys but can't open locks?" A piano: the riddle plays on two meanings of "keys".
fact: "Forwards I'm heavy, backwards I'm not": the answer is "ton", which reversed spells "not".
fact: The five-letter word "short" becomes "shorter" when you add two letters.
fact: "Dad's car rots" hides the vegetable CARROT across "car" and "rots".
fact: "The pilot terrified" hides the animal OTTER across "pilot" and "terrified".
fact: A lipogram leaves out one letter on purpose; "e" is the most common letter in English, so a lipogram without "e" is the hardest.
fact: Pear, pair and pare sound the same (they are homophones, words that sound alike but differ in spelling and meaning); peer has a different vowel sound.
fact: "Fast" can mean quick, and it can also mean going without food on purpose ("to fast").
fact: In the riddle "Brothers and sisters I have none, but this man's father is my father's son", "my father's son" can only be the speaker himself, so the man in the photo is his son.
fact: A sentence where every word starts with the same letter is alliteration taken to the limit, like "Six silly snakes slid slowly south, sipping soup."
fact: "Eejit" is an Irish word for idiot, often said in fun.
exercise: What has keys but can't open locks? | A piano | A front door | A car | A padlock
exercise: Why does the piano riddle work? | "Keys" has two different meanings | Pianos have locks inside them | Doors never have any keys | It doesn't make sense at all
exercise: "Forwards I'm heavy, backwards I'm not." What am I? | ton | not | stone | heavy
exercise: Which five-letter word gets "shorter" when you add two letters? | short | small | brief | quick
exercise: "Dad's car rots in the shed." Which vegetable is hidden? | carrot | pea | leek | onion
exercise: "The pilot terrified everyone." Which animal is hidden? | otter | rat | eel | owl
exercise: Which sentence has no letter e in it at all? | My dog is big and fluffy. | My dog loves long walks. | The dog is big and fluffy. | My dog runs and jumps here.
exercise: What is writing that leaves out one letter on purpose called? | A lipogram | A palindrome | A pangram | An anagram
exercise: Why is a lipogram without "e" so hard? | E is the commonest letter in English | E is the first letter of the alphabet | E is silent in most English words | E only ever turns up in long words
exercise: Which word does NOT sound like the others: pear, pair, pare, peer? | peer | pear | pair | pare
exercise: What word is spelled wrong in every dictionary? | wrong | dictionary | every | none
exercise: Which word means both "quick" and "going without food"? | fast | slow | hungry | rapid
exercise: "Brothers and sisters I have none, but this man's father is my father's son." Who is in the photo? | His son | Himself | His father | His brother
exercise: In that riddle, who is "my father's son"? | The speaker himself | The speaker's brother | The speaker's uncle | The man in the photo
exercise: Which sentence has at least eight words, all starting with the same letter? | Six silly snakes slid slowly south, sipping soup. | Six snakes slid south, sipping some soup. | Silly snakes slide. | Six silly snakes slid slowly, and they sipped soup.
exercise: Are "pear" and "pair" homophones? | Yes: they sound the same but differ | No: they are spelled the same way | No: they sound quite different | Yes: they mean exactly the same
test: Your mate: "Nothing's spelled wrong in a dictionary, innit, so the answer's 'nothing'." What is the trick? | The word "wrong" is spelled W-R-O-N-G | Dictionaries are really full of mistakes | The answer is the word "dictionary" | The riddle simply has no answer at all
test: Which animal is hidden across two words in "I saw a crab bit Tom"? | rabbit | crab | bat | cat
test: Which fruit is hidden in "The cap pleased Gran"? | apple | pea | grape | lemon
test: What would happen if you added "er" to "short"? | It would make "shorter", a longer word | It would make a word with fewer letters | It would stop being a real word | It would turn into a palindrome
test: A cartoon says pear, pair, pare and peer all sound exactly the same. Fix it. | "Peer" has a different vowel sound | They really do all sound the same | "Pear" is the odd one out | "Pair" is the odd one out
test: Tyler, rough: "Riddles are stupid, a piano's got no keys, it's got buttons, you muppet." ("Muppet" means fool.) Who is right? | The riddle: a piano's keys really are keys | Tyler: piano keys are really buttons | Both, since keys and buttons are one thing | Nobody, since pianos have locks and keys
test: Dylan, Gen Alpha: "I'm fasting till lunch, no cap, I'm starving." ("No cap" means no lie.) What does "fasting" mean here? | Not eating | Running quickly | Sleeping in | Hurrying up
test: Which word, written backwards, makes another real word? | ton | heavy | piano | pear
test: Which sentence leaves out the letter "a"? | The big dog slept in the sun. | A big dog slept in the sun. | The big cat slept in the sun. | The dog sat in the sun.
test: A man shows a photo: "Brothers and sisters I have none, but this man's father is my father's son." His friend says, "So it's you!" Why is the friend wrong? | Then the photo man's father would be his dad, not him | The man has a brother, so it is his brother | It is his father, so the friend is half right | The friend is right; the photo shows the man
test: Which phrase hides the name of a country across two words? | Mr Chin and his dog | Mr Lee and his cat | Mr Brown and his fox | Mr King and his hen
exam: Identify the term denoting a composition from which a particular letter has been deliberately excluded. | A lipogram | A pangram | A palindrome | An acrostic
exam: Ascertain the object that "has keys but cannot open locks" in the well-known riddle. | A piano | A door | A padlock | A car
exam: Select the word which, among "pear", "pair", "pare" and "peer", is not pronounced in the same manner as the others. | Peer | Pear | Pair | Pare
exam: It is given that a speaker has no siblings and states that "this man's father is my father's son". Determine the identity of the man depicted. | The speaker's son | The speaker himself | The speaker's father | The speaker's brother
